### Speaker Script


Slide - about data

After drafting the problem statement, it was important to understand the data that we were working with. Initially, we received **9 different datasets**, which together contained around **8,995 candidate records**. Among these, we identified **HR_Hiring_details_transaction.csv** as our primary dataset because it contained the core information related to the candidates and their hiring process. The remaining datasets acted as **supporting lookup and master datasets**, providing additional information such as joining status, location, region mapping, interview dates, line of business, domicile, and postal-code . To combine all this information, we used **Candidate_Ref and other relevant keys** to establish relationships between the datasets. We specifically followed a **LEFT JOIN strategy**, as our objective was to retain every candidate record from the primary dataset, even when some supporting information was missing. This helped us avoid losing candidate records during the data integration process. After combining the relevant information, we obtained **26 features**, with **Joining Status** as our target variable, where **81.30% of candidates were marked as Joined and 18.70% as Not Joined**. This gave us a consolidated view of the candidate and hiring data, which we then used for our further analysis.


